# Reading a file a line at a time, with boxes that remember nothing.
#
# The trick is that the reader's *whole state* — the file's text, how
# far in it has got, and the line it just took — is one value on one
# wire. `step` hands that value back to itself, so the next call
# learns where the last one stopped without anything being kept
# anywhere.
#
#   open ──> step ──greater──┬──> say
#                            └──> step   (round again)
#                 └─equal──> (nothing: the file is finished)
#
# `open` runs exactly once: its only port holds a constant, and a
# constant is never a ring buffer, so bring-up seeds it.
#
# `step` is a comparator, and what it compares is its own returned
# reader against a reader whose `more` is zero. So "greater" means
# "this call produced a line" and "equal" means "the text ran out".
# The equal exit is deliberately unwired — that is how the loop ends.

station open src/boxes/133-line-boxes.c:open_lines p
  in 0 = "/dev/shm/minimal-soramech/three-lines.txt"
  out 0 - step.0

station step src/boxes/133-line-boxes.c:next_line c
  out 0 $0
  in 0 - open.0
  in 0 - step.2
  in 1 = { "", 0, 0, "", 0 }
  out 2 - say.0
  out 2 - step.0

station say src/boxes/133-line-boxes.c:say_line p
  in 0 - step.2
